lly over  time in a small circuit and  have you looked at this and can you  model how how the  behavior changes in in development in  the crab  okay so for a number of years  pia meron first started doing  development of the  cells in the lobster stomatogastric  nervous system  and then for a number of years we worked  on development of the  pyloric motor patterns and gastric motor  patterns  in the lobster stomatogastric ganglion  so the story is that  fifty percent of the way through  embryonic development  the ganglion's active um  the the separation between the pyloric  and the gastric mill networks  is is not fully there um the  preparations are far less regular and  then  over time modulators come in and  everything settles down  so that by the time the animals go  through their first larval stage  they're generating pretty decent um  rhythms now the problem is that  lobsters the adult lobsters that you  guys all like to eat you're five to  seven years old  um and so they're not a great place to  do  development and you can't do  manipulations in development the same  way you could if you're working in a  flyer worm so we've done a fair bit of  work  and then we got it to a certain point  and then i just decided that  it was not the best preparation for  doing development  got it okay another question about  feedback into the network and that is  with regard to temperature  the question is do you believe that the  effect of temperature is purely passive  on the kinetics of the channels and  and and transmitter release or do you  think that there could also be  some modifying some sensing of  temperature  more centrally that then modifies the  behavior of the circuit  so in every animal that i know of there  are  neurons whose role is temperature  sensing  and that play a role often in  temperature preference  i think everything we've been studying  so far in the isolated  stomatogastric ganglion could be  accounted for  just on the on the behavior what you  call  passive temperature modulation but  i am sure in the animal there are  neurons whose function is temperature  sensing  as lobsters and crabs just like every  other animal i know  have mechanisms to to show temperature  preference and to follow temperature  gradients  i just don't think it's my sdg neurons  that are doing that for the animal  but enjoy they are somewhere that are  okay well thanks very much i think we  got to the general idea of most of the  questions and so i want to thank the  audience and  thanks eve for a very stimulating talk  and  and enjoy the rest of the evening the  session that's going on now  uh is on uh basically how do you  tell your story in science by liz nealy  the executive director of the  story collider so that's the next  presentation and again  thank you so much eve thanks everyone  for joining this  virtual brain investigative meeting  thank you bye guys  bye-bye


